{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:01:51.917Z","tool_name":"run_shell_command","tool_id":"run_shell_command__call_480703","parameters":{"description":"Find all stable sets of line type and triangle type for K=2..11.","command":"python3 -c '\ndef dominates(x, y):\n    return (x[0] > y[0] and x[1] > y[1]) or (x[0] > y[0] and x[2] > y[2]) or (x[1] > y[1] and x[2] > y[2])\n\nfor K in range(2, 12):\n    pts = [(a, b, K - a - b) for a in range(K + 1) for b in range(K + 1 - a)]\n    # Every internally stable set V has the property that every pair shares a coordinate.\n    # Let us check all subsets V of size <= 4 that don_t lie on a single line, AND all subsets of a single line!\n    # Wait: can an internally stable set have >= 4 points not on a single line?\n    # Suppose u, v share coord 0 (u0=v0=a), u, w share coord 1 (u1=w1=b), v, w share coord 2 (v2=w2=c).\n    # Wait: what if u=(a,b,K-a-b), v=(a,b2,c), w=(a2,b,c), and z=(a, b, K-a-b)? That is u!\n    # What if u, v, w all go through (a,b,c) with a+b+c != K? No, points are in the plane x+y+z=K!\n    # In the plane x+y+z=K, the lines x=a, y=b, z=c are three lines forming a triangle (unless a+b+c=K).\n    # The vertices of the triangle are:\n    # P1 = (a, b, K-a-b) (intersection of x=a, y=b)\n    # P2 = (a, K-a-c, c) (intersection of x=a, z=c)\n    # P3 = (K-b-c, b, c) (intersection of y=b, z=c)\n    # Every pair of points in V defines a line parallel to one of the 3 axes!\n    # Wait: in a plane with 3 families of parallel lines, what is a set of points where every pair lies on a line from one of the 3 families?\n    # Either all points lie on ONE line, OR there are at most 3 points forming a triangle with sides parallel to the 3 families!\n    \n    # Let us check all stable sets for K!\n    # Type 1: subsets of a line (say x_i = c)\n    # Type 2: subsets of {P1, P2, P3} of size <= 3.\n    stable = []\n    # Check lines\n    for coord in range(3):\n        for c in range(K + 1):\n            line = [p for p in pts if p[coord] == c]\n            # Wait: any subset of a line is internally stable!\n            # And adding more points only helps external stability!\n            # Wait: why wouldn_t we take the whole line? Wait: can a proper subset of a line be stable?\n            # No! Because internal stability is automatic for any subset of a line,Wait: if V is stable and V subset line, if x is in line \\ V, then x must be dominated by V!\n            # COULD x in line \\ V be dominated by V? NO! Because line is internally stable, NO element of line dominates x!\n            # So if V is a subset of a line, V MUST be the ENTIRE line!\n            ext = [y for y in pts if y not in line and not any(dominates(x, y) for x in line)]\n            if not ext:\n                stable.append((\"line\", coord, c, len(line)))\n    # Check size <= 3 not on a line\n    from itertools import combinations\n    for r in [1, 2, 3]:\n        for V in combinations(pts, r):\n            if len(set(p[0] for p in V)) == 1 or len(set(p[1] for p in V)) == 1 or len(set(p[2] for p in V)) == 1:\n                continue\n            if any(dominates(x, y) for x in V for y in V):\n                continue\n            ext = [y for y in pts if y not in V and not any(dominates(x, y) for x in V)]\n            if not ext:\n                stable.append((\"triangle\", V))\n    print(f\"K={K}: {stable}\")\n'"}}
