{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":"2026-10-23T04:02:10.663Z","tool_name":"update_topic","tool_id":"update_topic__call_565681","parameters":{"title":"Complete Characterization and Proof Verification","strategic_intent":"Share the complete characterization and proof outline on the board and verify all details.","summary":"I discovered a complete characterization of internal and external stability on the grid. Two allocations are incomparable under domination iff they share at least one coordinate. Using this, any internally stable set either lies on a single coordinate line (which fails external stability because of integer discreteness) or has size at most 3 forming a coordinate triangle, which only satisfies external stability when K is even and V = {(0, K/2, K/2), (K/2, 0, K/2), (K/2, K/2, 0)}. I am now posting this to the board to coordinate with opus."}}
